WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.939
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with Rachel Bernstein, who's an educator,

00:00:03.939 --> 00:00:11.600
therapist, author, media and government consultant,
and also host of the weekly podcast Indoctrination.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.800
And for over 30 years has been treating former
cult members and their families victims of

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.769
malignant narcissists, former supremacists,
conspiracy theorists, quite, quite a list.

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:24.760
Rachel, I appreciate you joining me today.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.580
Speaker 5: Oh, it's my pleasure.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:27.710
Thanks for asking me to be on.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:32.020
Speaker 1: So, I mean, before we get into
some of the nitty gritty, how did it happen

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.750
that you started?

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.200
I don't know if you would necessarily call
it a focus, but that you started working in

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.340
this particular space with these types of
folks.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.540
Speaker 5: Right.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.540
Yeah.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:44.360
It is most definitely a focus and it has remained
so.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:49.030
And there was someone in my family who got
ensnared in a particular cultic group.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.610
So it was kind of dinner table conversation.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:57.410
And then when I went off to college, I had
already learned about some cults that use

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:04.070
front names on college campuses to get people
off of college campuses and kind of just take

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:05.070
them away.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:06.070
Hmm.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.460
And I saw them with my own eyes.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.350
And, you know, first, when you hear about
cults, you're thinking someone's being a little

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.720
paranoid and maybe they're exaggerating.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.790
And there it was.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:20.720
And then when I went on to become a therapist,
I was noticing that there were a lot of people

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.640
who just didn't think they existed.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.259
And I thought, I need to be a resource.

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:29.790
I need to be able to be out there to help
people when they have had this issue, because

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.000
it's very specific.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:38.410
Speaker 1: When we talk about cults, for example,
to start with, there are dramatically different

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:44.829
scenarios when someone has already left the
cult and the support that they might need

00:01:44.829 --> 00:01:51.500
at that point versus if someone is in the
cult and loved ones may be thinking more of

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.330
what is sometimes colloquially known as an
intervention.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:02:02.039
Can you talk about whether you deal with both
or more of the after the fact only?

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:04.159
Speaker 5: Right.

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:08.050
So most of what I deal with is the after the
fact.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:14.930
Although I am involved in some interventions,
these are non enforceable conversational style

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:16.959
interventions.

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:21.640
It's important that people know that so that
they don't get afraid of them, that it's not

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.400
at all that you're, you know, pulling up in
a van and putting someone in something that's

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.569
windowless and taking them away.

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:28.569
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:32.540
Speaker 5: It really is helping to educate
them about what they are involved in because

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:37.610
people involved in cults actually know the
least about them.

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.370
They have the least amount of access to the
information.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.580
So all you're doing is informing them about
what's true.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:49.959
But I'd say about 70% of the people I work
with have been out sometimes for a few days.

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:55.702
I mean, I've had people show up in my office
as they left that day and others who have

00:02:55.702 --> 00:02:59.520
been out for years and realized that they
need help because there are so many triggers

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.930
that have been implanted that they didn't
realize until they got triggered.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:09.480
Speaker 1: When I've interviewed former white
supremacists, for example, one of the trends

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:15.680
is that when they reflect on how they were
originally sucked in, they they all tell a

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:16.950
very similar story.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:21.530
And it doesn't mean it's the only story, but
at least the ones I've spoken to have a similar

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:29.349
story, which is sort of opportunity caused
by usually problems at home, often with absent

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:37.209
parents or abusive parents, a sort of search
for meaning and inclusion from some kind of

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:38.209
group.

00:03:38.209 --> 00:03:39.650
So sometimes there was bullying at school.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:44.140
It's it's sort of circumstantial in terms
of the environment that they're in that makes

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:49.470
them vulnerable to the recruitment of some
of these supremacists.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:51.370
Is that your experience?

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:56.459
And also, is it similar with the people that
get sucked into cults that may not be extremist

00:03:56.459 --> 00:03:57.840
in that same way?

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.220
Speaker 5: Right.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.790
So that has been my experience as well.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:05.930
In addition to there is a timing factor.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:10.790
So when people ask me what kind of person
gets involved in a cult, sometimes it is exactly

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:12.930
that that they're looking for something.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:17.509
They're looking for meaning, they're looking
for connection community, for having a purpose.

00:04:17.509 --> 00:04:24.440
They're trying to find some space for them
in the world, maybe in kind of parallel universe

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.320
because they haven't found their way in mainstream
society.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.330
But for a lot of people, it's a timing issue.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:35.900
They are away from home for the first time
or they just went through a trauma or they

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:40.220
were just diagnosed with something, or they
were just abused for the first time and they're

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.020
looking to escape that.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.400
So I think a lot of factors actually play
into it.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:51.360
Speaker 1: You've worked with the FBI on some
cases that maybe some in our audience are

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:52.360
familiar with.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:56.800
One such cases, the Branch Davidians in Waco,
Texas, and the negotiations that took place

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:57.800
there.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.730
Can you talk a little bit about.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.590
What is such a negotiation process like?

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:07.600
What are the what are the principles that
are at play in such negotiations in those

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:08.600
situations?

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:13.750
Speaker 5: You know, those are interesting
times, certainly, because there is something

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:16.680
very incendiary about the moment.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.520
And so you're kind of trying to beat the clock.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:23.990
What you find when you're dealing with the
FBI or really any kind of government agency

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:28.141
is that you're trying to educate them about
what's different about this and how they need

00:05:28.141 --> 00:05:31.370
to interact in a different way than they might
be thinking.

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:32.430
That doesn't always happen.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.750
They don't always listen, unfortunately.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:41.530
But you can give your idea about how to make
sure that they don't make people afraid of

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:45.300
leaving by doing what they're doing to get
them to come out.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:49.800
I've been involved in a number of raids, not
planning them necessarily, but helping with

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:54.470
the aftermath and helping with people who
have been taken off compounds with the Branch

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:55.470
Davidians.

00:05:55.470 --> 00:06:01.900
I use the information that I knew about what
would make people scared of leaving and to

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:07.000
and I think to really make them feel scared
of the world, which is what unfortunately

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:14.120
the ATF did by playing very loud music and
making them really feel overwhelmed.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:21.080
And by kind of proving the cult leader right
about the world, unfortunately, that then

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:22.180
they stayed in.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:29.070
But the other information I used was from
people who had escaped before the fire.

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:33.830
Some of the families, the parents, the children
who had been able to get out, who were also

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:38.710
guiding me about what they thought would be
the best sort of course of action for the

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:40.830
people still in it.

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:43.289
Sometimes they listen again and sometimes
not so much.

00:06:43.289 --> 00:06:50.259
Speaker 1: So you mentioned you mentioned
the leaders, depending on which of which cult

00:06:50.259 --> 00:06:53.509
one is talking about or extremist group.

00:06:53.509 --> 00:07:00.949
Oftentimes the followers allow or may be accept
is the better word.

00:07:00.949 --> 00:07:04.229
That their leaders play by a different set
of rules.

00:07:04.229 --> 00:07:09.110
There's rules for the followers, and the leaders
often don't follow those very same rules.

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:16.479
Now, in cases like maybe a Jim Jones and the
People's Temple, it's he's seen sort of like

00:07:16.479 --> 00:07:18.300
as a deity almost.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.319
And so that makes it really easy to understand.

00:07:20.319 --> 00:07:24.861
Well, like, if your God or God, like, of course,
you have a different set of rules that you

00:07:24.861 --> 00:07:27.330
play by than we the followers play by.

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:31.449
But not all of these cults have leaders that
necessarily frame themselves in this kind

00:07:31.449 --> 00:07:33.229
of deified way.

00:07:33.229 --> 00:07:37.569
In the non overtly deifying cults.

00:07:37.569 --> 00:07:42.419
What are the explanations or justifications
that the followers have for why the rules

00:07:42.419 --> 00:07:46.039
they have to follow aren't the rules that
the leader has to follow?

00:07:46.039 --> 00:07:48.110
Speaker 5: It's a great question.

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:49.351
It happens time and time again.

00:07:49.351 --> 00:07:54.789
And to make this very relatable, this is something
that also happens in relationships with controllers

00:07:54.789 --> 00:07:59.879
and with malignant narcissists, where they
can do whatever they want and you can't question

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:05.580
them and they can have access to your information
or your passwords or your, you know, ATM.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:08.229
And they they can gain everything.

00:08:08.229 --> 00:08:09.360
And you have nothing.

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:10.509
You have no power.

00:08:10.509 --> 00:08:14.289
You can't get any of their information, but
they can get all of yours.

00:08:14.289 --> 00:08:19.139
So it happens in these sort of one on one,
these sort of dyad cults, too, within these

00:08:19.139 --> 00:08:20.389
relationships.

00:08:20.389 --> 00:08:25.949
But going back to the idea of cults and why
permission is given, permission is given for

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:30.330
everything when it comes to the leader within
a cult.

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:34.250
And that's actually why some people start
cults, because they love that.

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:38.440
They love having the freedom to do or say
whatever they want, and they love knowing

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:40.479
that the rules only apply to the followers.

00:08:40.479 --> 00:08:47.200
I mean, it's sort of, you know, the sociopaths
or the narcissistic playground and, you know,

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:48.920
they couldn't enjoy it more.

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:54.000
Unfortunately, what happens when you get involved
in a system like this is that there's kind

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.490
of what I see as a dual track of influence.

00:08:57.490 --> 00:08:59.399
One is overt and one is covert.

00:08:59.399 --> 00:09:02.079
And the overt is here are the teachings.

00:09:02.079 --> 00:09:06.350
Here's what we believe in and here's what
you need to do to be a good follower.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:12.670
The covert one is if you question how things
are run, you will not receive the benefit

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:13.839
of this.

00:09:13.839 --> 00:09:16.720
All of your sacrifice will be for naught,
right?

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:18.290
Your time is going to be wasted.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:25.260
If you are questioning the fact that I have
44 Rolls-Royces, like with Rajneesh, and you

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:29.240
get nothing and you sleep on the floor, then
that negativity is going to get in the way

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:32.880
of you receiving what I'm trying to give you.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:35.510
And you need to question yourself.

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:40.310
People learn how to turn the focus inward
within a cult, and they question themselves

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:43.490
for doubting and doubting and being negative.

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:49.520
Being angry about anything or resenting anything
in a cult is considered really, really awful.

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:53.519
And it's going to be counterproductive to
what you're trying to get by being there.

00:09:53.519 --> 00:09:57.480
So you think you're working against yourself
by having critical thinking?

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:01.020
It's a fascinating kind of way of getting
into gaslighting.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:06.870
Speaker 1: When I've spoken to experts on
conspiracy theories, sometimes the idea comes

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:12.649
up of like more destructive or dangerous conspiracy
theories versus ones, ones that are sort of

00:10:12.649 --> 00:10:13.660
like more benign.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:18.060
And sometimes there will be this contrast
where it's like, you know, some of the anti-Semitic

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:22.870
conspiracy theories that lead people to seek
out and do violence against Jewish people.

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:23.870
Very dangerous.

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:27.360
On the other hand, believing the earth is
flat, not so dangerous.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:32.670
And to some degree, every conspiracy theory
expert that I talked to says, yes, in the

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:35.970
overt sense it's true that one is much more
dangerous than the other.

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:40.380
At the same time, rarely is there only one
conspiratorial belief.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.190
These things open doors to more and more extreme
beliefs.

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:46.690
And so we should be careful to say this one
is totally benign.

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:48.589
We don't need to worry about it.

00:10:48.589 --> 00:10:56.139
When we think about that as applied to cults,
are there what we might call not very dangerous

00:10:56.139 --> 00:10:57.139
cults?

00:10:57.139 --> 00:11:02.389
Like, for example, I read an article not long
ago about how CrossFit or like Peloton or

00:11:02.389 --> 00:11:08.990
SoulCycle, they have a lot of the elements
on paper of a cult, but it's not really dangerous

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:11.040
in the way that a lot of these others are.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:14.279
Can you talk a little bit about that and kind
of categorizing these?

00:11:14.279 --> 00:11:15.510
Speaker 5: Right.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:20.089
So yeah, there are some cult groups that are
more dangerous than others, undoubtedly.

00:11:20.089 --> 00:11:22.440
And it all depends upon the leader.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:27.079
It depends where the leader wants to take
it and what the leader needs in terms of ego

00:11:27.079 --> 00:11:28.279
aggrandizement.

00:11:28.279 --> 00:11:32.810
If they want to just test you to see what
you're willing to do for them because it makes

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:36.450
them feel powerful, then they will sometimes
push you to the brink.

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:40.510
They'll push you to the point where it's not
healthy for you, it's actually dangerous for

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:45.050
you just because they want to see you struggling
because they told you to.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:50.370
And I think that when you're dealing with
cults that are dangerous, you're dealing with

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:55.779
cults where they will tell you, for example,
like a lot of the ones that deal with pseudoscience,

00:11:55.779 --> 00:11:57.820
where here we have the cure for you.

00:11:57.820 --> 00:12:00.170
So people bypass actual cures.

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:04.750
Things that could really help them in order
to get what the cult leader or that person

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:10.560
is telling them they need and they end up
not doing well or they end up not living,

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:12.880
but they've already signed their rights away.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:15.520
And so there's nothing that they can do about
it.

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:21.600
And so, yes, when you're engaging in anything
that feels like someone's giving you the cure,

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:23.730
it can be potentially very, very dangerous.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:27.820
And there can be a lot of medical neglect
actually in those situations.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:33.890
The other is when people are finding themselves
getting more militaristic and they're talking

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:36.860
about the end times, they're talking about
survival.

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:43.459
That's when you get into a lot more of the
stockpiling of weapons of also needing to

00:12:43.459 --> 00:12:48.621
see the world and the people in it as less
than as not human anymore, and that you're

00:12:48.621 --> 00:12:52.190
above them when you have that polarization
and you have a lot of danger.

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:57.000
Speaker 1: Last thing generally that I want
to talk about and there's a lot here is get

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:58.420
getting people out right.

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:04.699
We know when it comes to false beliefs, generally
beating people over the head with facts doesn't

00:13:04.699 --> 00:13:05.699
really work very well.

00:13:05.699 --> 00:13:10.250
You know, we've had dozens of guests where
we talk about that in one way, shape or form.

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:11.399
Empathy is good.

00:13:11.399 --> 00:13:12.720
Understanding is good.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:15.899
Communicating that the person's not going
to be shamed if they decide, Oh, you know

00:13:15.899 --> 00:13:16.971
what, I was wrong about this thing.

00:13:16.971 --> 00:13:22.910
I want to get out making clear that they're
going to have the resources with Scientologists.

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:24.290
That's the big thing where they have nothing.

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:29.370
And so part of staying in is I'm going to
have literally nothing if I get out of here.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:34.949
In terms of the getting out, you mentioned
sometimes the people in the cult know the

00:13:34.949 --> 00:13:37.000
least about the cult.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:40.590
I don't necessarily need to.

00:13:40.590 --> 00:13:45.149
One thing I sometimes think about is in conversations
with Mormons and there's different opinions

00:13:45.149 --> 00:13:46.620
about whether Mormonism is a cult.

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:51.459
But let's at least consider the possibility
it might be cult like many Mormons.

00:13:51.459 --> 00:13:56.560
Don't know that Joseph Smith, each time he
read the gold tablets or whatever they are.

00:13:56.560 --> 00:14:00.380
It was a different reading and he had a history
of being a con man and all these different

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:02.520
things that they just don't know that.

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:08.769
And simply telling them that that's a reality
is not going to be convincing if you just

00:14:08.769 --> 00:14:10.660
kind of give them that information.

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:15.790
So what are the right ways to communicate
the information that they might be lacking?

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:19.279
Because we know, just like, hey, here's a
bunch of stuff about your beliefs doesn't

00:14:19.279 --> 00:14:20.279
really work.

00:14:20.279 --> 00:14:21.279
Speaker 5: Right?

00:14:21.279 --> 00:14:25.220
I'm so glad you asked this question because
this is most of the work that I do helping

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:29.720
to have the conversation that's difficult
to have with someone who might not be ready

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:34.880
to look at the fact that they were involved
in a cult and also helping guide families

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:40.270
and friends and spouses to know how to have
these conversations so as not to kind of,

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:43.649
you know, push them further back in and make
them feel alienated, judged, etc..

00:14:43.649 --> 00:14:44.649
Right.

00:14:44.649 --> 00:14:50.350
So one of the things you want to be able to
do is you want to engage them in a conversation

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:51.880
about what they liked about it.

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:55.850
Let them know that you're going to be fair
minded about this, that there must have been

00:14:55.850 --> 00:14:57.460
a reason that they got involved.

00:14:57.460 --> 00:14:59.070
What was the promise?

00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:00.500
What did they think they could get?

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:03.009
And maybe who did they meet, who they liked,
etc.?

00:15:03.009 --> 00:15:08.140
So that you're not just going in for the kill,
you're not just going in to talk about what

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:11.630
was bad, because if you do, then they're going
to feel foolish.

00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:14.660
They're going to feel like if it was all bad,
why did I get involved?

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:15.660
Why did I stay?

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:16.660
Yeah.

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:19.961
So talk about what they got now or really
talk about what they were promised that they

00:15:19.961 --> 00:15:24.110
probably still didn't get because there's
always the carrot that's dangled.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:28.910
So you get a sense of sort of what they were
searching for, what is part of their value

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:33.529
system, what they wanted to cure in the world,
what they wanted to cure about themselves,

00:15:33.529 --> 00:15:40.230
and then let them know that you have information
and let them know that they can listen to

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:45.889
it or not they can disagree with it or not,
but that this is information you want to give

00:15:45.889 --> 00:15:50.610
to them out of respect for them, that this
was something that was kept from them.

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:55.570
But people should have access to information
so they can make a fully informed decision.

00:15:55.570 --> 00:16:00.830
And you let them know that if they feel like
they want to go back, they can go back there.

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:04.360
There have been plenty of times that I've
done an intervention where the person has

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:05.360
gone back.

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:09.040
But because I said, Listen, I'm glad you're
saying that you're not involved in a cult

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:10.459
just in case.

00:16:10.459 --> 00:16:15.079
Let me just give you a rundown of what a cult
is and what happens there.

00:16:15.079 --> 00:16:17.810
And they could disagree with me in the moment,
but they went back.

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:20.050
It's like I planted a seed.

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:23.270
They went back and they saw these things for
themselves so they could come out on their

00:16:23.270 --> 00:16:24.800
own terms.

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:29.130
And so if the person doesn't feel judged,
if they really are able to maintain the sense

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:33.930
that you see them as capable, you see them
as bright, you see them as people who are

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:39.079
not just gullible people, then you are going
to be able to have a much more successful

00:16:39.079 --> 00:16:42.550
conversation and then say, I have information.

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:46.690
You can also share me and share information
with me about your group, and I will share

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:51.399
information about it too, that I've heard
so that there really is a reciprocity to the

00:16:51.399 --> 00:16:52.399
conversation.

00:16:52.399 --> 00:16:54.070
Speaker 1: Super, super interesting.

00:16:54.070 --> 00:16:58.860
We've been speaking with Rachel Bernstein,
who is an educator, therapist and author.

00:16:58.860 --> 00:16:59.929
She also.

00:16:59.929 --> 00:17:02.080
Hosts the weekly podcast Indoctrination.

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:04.110
Rachel, really a pleasure having you on.

00:17:04.110 --> 00:17:05.310
I appreciate your time.

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:06.310
Speaker 5: Thank you so much for having me
on.

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:06.315
Good to talk to you.

